k at what's  going on in france right now for example  given that they're so dependent on  nuclear at present even though they say  they want to reduce that and they are  expecting to reduce that quite  significantly as a percent of overall  energy use  nevertheless they are going to be  dependent on  not just the power stations they have  now but new ones as well  and that's the real issue because quite  a lot of the old ones uh are coming to  the end of their useful life so i'm  afraid we are going to have new nuclear  as well in the mix even though there are  still serious concerns about whether  because they are environmentally  friendly  well it's clear that they're not  environmentally friendly they've been  trying to find a solution to the waste  problem a toxic waste problem for 70  years without without coming up with one  but the other problem with in terms of  climate change it's very clear to build  a new nuclear power station from scratch  to it producing any electricity takes 20  years  that would mean that if we started today  to be the mid-2040s before any nuclear  power stations start helping us reduce  our carbon emissions which is 22 years  too late what we need to be doing is  concentrating on three things energy  efficiency investing in renewables and  investing in modern  storage technologies those are the three  investments that europe should be  looking at which the united kingdom  should be looking at  okay let's move on to our next topic  shall we now because it might be a  choice between heating and eating we  were warned today as energy bills look  set to rocket in the spring now some  energy suppliers and conservative mps  have said that one solution will be to  suspend vat and green taxes which are  currently levied on our bills so are  they right and donaka the figures are  quite startling aren't they the average  household energy bill could rise by  723 pounds per year that's one estimate  so  should the green taxes go in order to  help out consumers  yeah it's quite shocking and is going to  put a lot of people into fuel poverty  and what we have here is a failure to  invest in energy efficiency and  renewables for 20 years by the uk  government that's left us at the mercy  of the fossil fuel markets it's fossil  fuels which are exploding in cost not by  a factor of nine so what we should be  looking at is cutting green taxes we've  been cutting our investment in cutting  bills in the future cutting our noses by  our faces so what we should be looking  at is actually look  um if we cut example vat that means  you're giving a tax subsidy to the  middle classes and to the rich what we  should be doing is cutting is restoring  the universal credit cut that the  government did so poor people have the  money to pay the extra cost in um uh  fuel that it will be exploding over the  next few months  do you think that's the right solution  vicky and that green taxes should be  kept  i think what the government is to do is  intervene and intervene pretty fast  because there is indeed a crucial issue  as to whether people are going to be  able to afford the energy and that they  need to consume in their homes and  whether they'll be able to survive if  you like and this you know heating or  eating is actually a very important  issue for a very large number of people  and even you know if you look at the  middle classes which  of course you know tend to be a bit more  sheltered actually the increase is going  to be pretty substantial so uh what you  can do of course is keep those taxes  still there uh if you look at the  percentage that goes for what we call  government environmental and social  schemes it's about 13 percent and v80 is  about five percent is five percent  actually for household bills it's  slightly higher for well i said 20 for  um  business users except for those that  don't use an awful lot of energy when  it's five percent so they're not huge uh  and of course as prices are going up uh  they are you know substantial if you  look at the percentages but overall  what's really happening is that this  wholesale price for gas that's going up  very significantly has gone up but the  question is how long will that last i  think there is a short-term issue to  medium-term issue that the government  needs to meet which is basically  assisting energy companies to keep those  uh increases that have to come at some  point down as much as possible and  looking at the price cap as well the way  it goes up in april so we don't get  those very substantial increases what  we've seen in various other countries is  that they have intervened they have done  something about vat they have done  something about some of those social  payments so actually i think government  needs to intervene very very  significantly  do you come in yeah the other the other  point is larger than green taxes is the  standing charges the standing charges  around 200 pounds on a gas electricity  bill is a tax on the poor why should a  poor pensioner living in a small flat  one room flat by ourselves pay the same  amount in standing charge as a massive  as a massive palace for a billionaire  but this they should be strapped and  that should be transferred to the to the  um bills themselves so that poor people  are not actually hammered by it so i'm  suggesting two things for poor people  restore the environmental credit cut and  actually abolish the standing charges so  poor people are not hammered by their  energy bills or the coming months  okay well done mccarthy and uh vicky  price i suspect this is something we  will return to in future weeks and  months but for the moment thanks both  very much indeed  thank you  now tomorrow show i'm going to be joined  by the chief executive of the climate  group helen clarkson and climate  scientist dr ella gilbert so do join me  for that meanwhile thanks for watching  and i'll see you next time  [Music]
 

